erage. Stock-based compensation of $37K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Phillips Edison & Company, Inc. is a mid-cap real estate business with a $5.7B market cap on $727M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +8% over the past three years and +12% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at -10% against a 6% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 8.9\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 2.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 76% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.7568348712365534},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.0) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"ROIC sits in the bottom decile among Real Estate peers \u2014 no moat advantage to speak of","sev":"red","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Interest-rate sensitivity and tenant-credit risk \u2014 cap rates and refinancing costs move faster than the 10-year leases can abso